####################################################################
# approximates_bridge.dlog — HAND-AUTHORED. NOT GENERATED.
#
# RDFox Datalog port of FRAMEWORK/KNOWLEDGE_BASE/inference.ttl's 14
# mda:approximates class-level owl:Restriction axioms (lines 520-614:
# `metric:X rdfs:subClassOf [owl:onProperty mda:approximates;
# owl:hasValue physiologicalProperty:Y]`) — a closed, enumerable,
# one-hop set, not a general reasoning problem.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: mint.py originally derived mda:approximates via a
# full owlrl.DeductiveClosure pass PER ALARM ARRIVAL (mint.clinical_
# context, now deleted) — measured at ~2.8-4s/call, because OWL-RL
# closure re-derives the WHOLE ~3000-triple static graph from scratch on
# every call, independent of how small the actual delta is. At the
# project's 14M-alarm target that is over a decade of serial compute —
# the exact per-call cost the RDFox migration was undertaken to
# eliminate in the first place (see op_knowledge.py's own profiling
# comments). Since the underlying axioms are all simple one-hop
# type-to-value mappings, RDFox's own Datalog engine derives them
# natively and incrementally — no Python-side reasoning needed at all.
#
# mda:administers/mda:targetsProcess (the other two mda:situational-
# tagged predicates the old clinical_context() also derived) are NOT
# ported here: no current rule references either. (Since the 2026-09
# data sync mint.py does ground administers/targetsProcess from the
# catalogue — see its module docstring — but nothing consumes them.)
#
# GRAPH SCOPING: ?g is bound on BOTH sides of every rule below, not left
# ungraphed. The metric's rdf:type triple lives in the alarm's own
# transient graph (mda:Metric is mda:nodeKind mda:Stateful, so
# mint.ground_chain's condition half — folded into insert_alarm's
# transient_triples — is where it lands, not the persistent graph).
# Binding ?g this way ties the derived approximates fact to that SAME
# graph's lifecycle, so it disappears the instant the metric's own value
# does — exactly what its consumer assumes (cat2a_process_priority.dlog
# and its aggregate rewrite reference `[?metric, mda:approximates,
# ?property] ?gN` as a graphed pattern). The clinical-event rules no
# longer use approximates at all (engine/clinical_events.py).
#
# Loaded UNCONDITIONALLY (like FRAMEWORK_FILES, not gated by
# poc_entry.py's enabled_rules) — this is prerequisite infrastructure,
# not an optional domain rule a user would ever want to disable.
####################################################################

@prefix mda:                  <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#> .
@prefix metric:                <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric/> .
@prefix physiologicalProperty: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/physiological-property/> .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:SaO2] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:SpO2] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:ArterialPressure] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:ArterialBloodPressure] ?g .

# See RespirationVolume's own comment below for why this rule (an
# uncovered rdfs:subClassOf child, found the same way) was missing.
[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:ArterialPressure] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:ArterialBloodPressure_Mean] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:VenousPressure] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:VenousBloodPressure] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:TissuePerfusion] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:PulseFlowIndex] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:ElectricalHeartRate] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:HeartRate] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:PulseRate] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:PulseRate] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:PaCO2] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:CO2] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationRate] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:RespirationRate] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationVolume] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:RespirationVolume] ?g .

# The 3 rules below were MISSING — found and fixed while implementing
# CAT3b (reduced_pulmonary_function.dlog matches metric:
# RespirationVolume_Minute specifically, never the broader
# metric:RespirationVolume, so it silently never fired at all: RDFox does
# NOT apply rdfs:subClassOf entailment automatically the way the original
# OWL-RL reasoner did (confirmed — see this project's own established
# practice of hand-porting every OWL construct RDFox doesn't natively
# reason over, e.g. this file's own header). inference.ttl's own comment
# (its RespirationVolume section) says as much: "RespirationVolume_Minute/
# _Tidal need no bridge of their own — same rdfs:subClassOf
# metric:RespirationVolume" — true for an OWL-RL closure, false for a
# direct one-hop Datalog port with no subclass reasoning behind it.
# Confirmed by systematically checking EVERY covered base type above for
# an uncovered rdfs:subClassOf child in data/vocab_generated.ttl — found 3
# gaps total, all fixed here: RespirationVolume_Minute/_Tidal (this rule),
# ArterialBloodPressure_Mean, and AirwayPressure_EndExpiratory (both
# below, next to their own base type's rule). This silently narrowed
# EVERY rule reading `mda:approximates` for an alarm carrying one of these
# 5 subtypes — not just reduced_pulmonary_function.dlog: cat2a's
# aggregate/on-demand branches read `?metric mda:approximates ?property`
# for ANY metric type, so a real cat2a-eligible alarm typed with one of
# these 5 subtypes was ALSO silently unmatchable before this fix — a
# live production gap, not merely a CAT3b blocker.
[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationVolume] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:RespirationVolume_Minute] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationVolume] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:RespirationVolume_Tidal] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationPressure] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:AirwayPressure] ?g .

# See RespirationVolume's own comment above for why this rule (an
# uncovered rdfs:subClassOf child, found the same way) was missing.
[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationPressure] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:AirwayPressure_EndExpiratory] ?g .

# AirwayPressure_Peak (2026-09-21, decision 39): the Ppeak / Paw-high
# alarms, previously plain AirwayPressure. The former metric:CPAP rule is
# gone with that metric (decision 38: CPAP alarms are
# AirwayPressure_EndExpiratory, covered above).
[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:RespirationPressure] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:AirwayPressure_Peak] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:Temperature] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:AirTemperature] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:Temperature] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:Temperature] ?g .

[?m, mda:approximates, physiologicalProperty:Humidity] ?g :-
    [?m, rdf:type, metric:Humidity] ?g .

####################################################################
# VALIDATION — to be executed against real RDFox before deleting
# mint.clinical_context: diff this file's derived approximates triples
# for at least one CAT2a fixture against clinical_context's own output,
# to confirm the Datalog rule reproduces the same fact the OWL-RL call
# did (not just that the regression suite's pass/fail count matches).
####################################################################
